<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project>

  <!--
    src/Directory.Build.props — product-code analyzers.

    Every project under src/ is product code: the layers the architecture
    rules police, the code that ships in the CLI, the desktop shell and the
    IPC endpoints. The analyzers that only make sense for product code live
    here rather than in the root file, because the root file is inherited by
    tests/ and samples/ as well — and those have their own async culture
    (174 `catch (Exception)` constructions, fixtures that block on purpose)
    and already lift TreatWarningsAsErrors.

    The root file is imported FIRST, exactly as apps/Directory.Build.props
    does it, so the shared property groups, the shared analyzer package
    references and the /BannedSymbols.txt wiring apply here unchanged. The
    import is what keeps this file additive: a property set in the root file
    is still set for every project under src/.

    WHY A SEPARATE FILE AT ALL
    MSBuild imports the NEAREST Directory.Build.props and stops. Before this
    file existed, the nearest one for a src/ project was the repository root,
    so a package that must not reach tests/ had no home: the root file would
    have handed it to every test project, and the apps/ file was too far up
    the tree to reach src/.

    MICROSOFT.VISUALSTUDIO.THREADING.ANALYZERS (vs-threading, 18.12.1) — REMOVED (#2109)

    The family was declared here and in apps/Directory.Build.props. It is
    gone, and the reason is the measurement rather than a preference:

      * 103.7 analyzer-seconds on the whole build (run 39527271843,
        `-p:ReportAnalyzer=true`) — 16 % of every analyzer-second in the
        tree, and the third largest cost after the SDK's own ILLink
        analyzer and Sonar.
      * 85.8 of those seconds — 83 % — are ONE rule,
        `VSTHRD010MainThreadUsageAnalyzer`, and
        `dotnet_diagnostic.VSTHRD010.severity = none` in .editorconfig.
        A rule at `severity = none` is silenced; the analyzer that
        implements it still runs over every compilation. So 83 % of this
        family was a rule that reports nothing, bought at the price of a
        rule that reports nothing.
      * Of the remaining rules, the only one this repository ever asked
        for is VSTHRD110 ("observe the result of async calls"), and it is
        at `suggestion` — which `TreatWarningsAsErrors` does NOT promote.
        The four rules of the package that ship as Error (VSTHRD004,
        VSTHRD011, VSTHRD107, VSTHRD109) are all `none` here, and a
        large part of the family is written against JoinableTaskFactory,
        which this repository does not reference. So no rule of this
        package could fail a build, before or after the removal.
      * The rule that DOES decide "is this an unobserved Task?" is
        HARCH003, a Roslyn analyzer in
        tests/Harbor.Architecture.Tests/Roslyn/UnobservedTaskAnalyzers.cs,
        which decides it by resolving the TYPE rather than by matching a
        call's spelling (#1852). HARCH003 is a Warning, so under
        TreatWarningsAsErrors=true it fails the build — it is STRICTLY
        STRONGER than the `suggestion` this package was asked for. The
        arch suite runs inside the Release build through the
        HarborArchitectureGate target, and again in the `test (arch)`
        job.

    The severity table keeps its 29 VSTHRD rows (in .editorconfig) even
    though the package is no longer referenced: they are inert today, and
    they are the table that would apply if the package came back — four
    of its rules ship as Error, so a bare re-reference would turn the
    tree red.
  -->

  <!--
    THE ANALYZER SCOPE OF THIS TREE (issue #1801). The scope table is
    tools/analyzer-scope.json and the gate over it is
    tools/check-analyzer-scope.py; this block is what the gate reads.

    BEFORE the import, not after: the root file declares every analyzer
    package with a Condition that reads this property, and MSBuild evaluates
    that Condition while the root file is read. A property set after the
    import would come too late — the item would already have been created
    or skipped.

    Only the families that DIFFER from the root default are stated here: a
    family that is not mentioned inherits the root's value, so the block
    says what this tree changes and nothing else.

    The four families turned ON here are the ones whose findings are
    PRODUCT findings, or whose absence would leave a product gate
    unwatched:

      AsyncFixer      five rules, all at `suggestion`. It cannot fail a
                      build anywhere, so it is kept where the shipped code
                      is and dropped from the test tree.
      BannedApi       RS0030 over BannedSymbols.txt and
                      BannedSymbols.DynamicCode.txt. The root props
                      NoWarn it in tests/ and samples/, so it produced
                      nothing there.
      DiLifetime      the DI gates (DI003/DI013/DI015/DI017/DI019 at
                      `error`) are the composition root's gate, and the
                      registrations live here and under apps/.
      Cfe             CFE0001 is the ROP-001 guard: a `.Value` on a failed
                      Result<T>. The root props NoWarn it in tests/ and
                      samples/, so it produced nothing there.

    Sonar is NOT turned on here: measured on this tree, all 324 of its
    findings are under tests/ and the product tree produces none. See
    docs/ANALYZERS.md §Analyzer scope for the measurement and for what the
    narrowing costs.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <HarborAnalyzerAsyncFixer>on</HarborAnalyzerAsyncFixer>
    <HarborAnalyzerDiLifetime>on</HarborAnalyzerDiLifetime>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Imported by RELATIVE path, not by the GetPathOfFileAbove function: the
       project-file analyzer (#1857) can only follow an import it can resolve on
       disk, and a property function is not resolvable to it, so the properties
       pinned in the root props (NuGetAuditMode, NuGetAuditLevel) look unset and
       the rules that read them fire. Same shape as tests/ and apps/. -->
  <Import Project="../Directory.Build.props"/>

  <!--
    Harbor.Arch.Analyzers — the repository's OWN architecture rules, as
    compiler extensions (#1842).

    A rule that lives in the architecture test project is judged by a test
    run; a rule that lives here is judged by the BUILD, over every product
    project, at the moment the code is written. The two rules that moved
    here are the ones whose invariant is SEMANTIC — a namespace root has an
    owner, and a dynamic-code API is a type — and a semantic invariant
    cannot be decided by reading the text of a file.

    OutputItemType=Analyzer hands the assembly to the compiler;
    ReferenceOutputAssembly=false keeps it out of the consuming project's
    reference list, so nothing about it reaches a shipped binary and the
    NativeAOT rule (#626), which governs shipped code, is not engaged.

    The reference is stated with an absolute path because this file is
    imported by EVERY project under src/, and a relative path in a props
    file is relative to the importing PROJECT, not to the props file.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Harbor.Arch.Analyzers/Harbor.Arch.Analyzers.csproj"
                      Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' != 'Harbor.Arch.Analyzers'">
      <OutputItemType>Analyzer</OutputItemType>
      <ReferenceOutputAssembly>false</ReferenceOutputAssembly>
    </ProjectReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    HARCH001 — the dynamic-code ban — is exempted for the plugin family, BY
    ID, and the predicate is the one the compiler-side ban file already
    uses: src/Harbor.Plugins.* loads code at run time because loading code
    at run time IS the plugin product.

    The exemption is by id and not by excluding the whole analyzer, so the
    other rule the analyzer carries still applies to those projects: one
    rule switched off must not switch off its neighbour.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.StartsWith('$(HarborDynamicCodeBanExemptProjectNamePrefix)'))">
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);HARCH001</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
